[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generic Classes, Interfaces, and Functions in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics are one of the most powerful features of TypeScript. They allow you to write reusable, flexible, and type-safe components by enabling you to pass types as parameters. Instead of locking down your code to a specific data type, generics let you work with multiple types while maintaining strict compile-time type checking."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Functions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A generic function uses a type parameter in its signature, typically denoted by the letter T. This allows the function to accept arguments of any type and return a value of that same type without resorting to the 'any' type."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"function identity<T>(arg: T): T {
    return arg;
}

let outputString = identity<string>('Hello Generics');
let outputNumber = identity<number>(42);"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, calling identity with a string infers the type automatically, so you can often omit the explicit type argument like identity('Hello Generics')."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Interfaces"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interfaces can also be made generic to describe data shapes that can vary based on the type of data they hold. This is extremely common when dealing with API responses or data containers."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"interface ApiResponse<T> {
    status: number;
    message: string;
    data: T;
}

interface User {
    id: number;
    name: string;
}

const userResponse: ApiResponse<User> = {
    status: 200,
    message: 'Success',
    data: { id: 1, name: 'Alice' }
};"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Classes"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generic classes allow you to create data structures, such as queues, stacks, or lists, that can store elements of any type while preserving type safety across all class methods and properties."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"class DataCollection<T> {
    private items: T[] = [];

    addItem(item: T): void {
        this.items.push(item);
    }

    getItems(): T[] {
        return this.items;
    }
}

const stringCollection = new DataCollection<string>();
stringCollection.addItem('TypeScript');
stringCollection.addItem('Generics');

const items = stringCollection.getItems();"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Constraints"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you want to write a generic function or class that works only with types that meet a specific set of criteria. You can use the 'extends' keyword to constrain your type parameters."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface HasLength {
    length: number;
}

function logLength<T extends HasLength>(arg: T): T {
    console.log(arg.length);
    return arg;
}

logLength('This is a string');
logLength([1, 2, 3, 4]);
// logLength(123); // Error: number does not have a length property"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generics improve code reusability by avoiding code duplication across similar data types."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"They preserve full type safety, preventing runtime errors associated with the 'any' type."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type constraints allow you to enforce specific properties or methods on generic type parameters."}]
